# <%= app_name.titleize %>

## Commands

```sh
bin/dev                      # Start the server, the Tailwind watcher and the jobs
bin/rails test               # Run the unit, controller and integration tests
bin/rails test:system        # Run the system tests
bin/rails test test/models/user_test.rb:42   # Run one test
bin/rubocop -A               # Fix style. Run it after each change to Ruby files
bin/brakeman                 # Scan for security problems
```

## Workflow

- Before you do work, say how to verify it.
- After a change, run the related tests and `bin/rubocop`.
- Never commit to `main`. Work on a feature branch.
- Never commit unless the owner asks. One commit for each completed task.
- A commit message is one line: a subject and nothing else.
- Explain the approach before a large change.
- Keep pull requests small and about one topic.

## Testing

Minitest, `factory_bot` and parallel tests. Factories are in `test/factories/`.
Use `create` and `build` directly, never `FactoryBot.create`.

### Work test-first

Before you change code, run only a new test.

- **An existing test covers the change, and the behaviour stays the same:**
  do not run it first. Make the change, then run that test.
- **No test covers the change:** write a new test. Run it and see it fail.
  Make the change. Run it and see it pass.
- **The change changes behaviour:** do not edit the existing test. Write a new
  test for the new behaviour. Run it and see it fail. Make the change. Run both
  tests. The existing test must now fail and the new test must pass. Then
  update or delete the existing test so that the suite passes. Report each
  existing test that you changed or deleted, and the behaviour that it tested.
- **A bug fix:** the new test must fail against the code before the fix. If
  that is not practical, say so. Do not assume that the test would have caught
  the bug.

### A failing test is a question

- Before you change anything, decide whether the code or the expectation is
  wrong, and say which.
- Never make a failing test pass by weakening it. Do not loosen a selector,
  widen a matcher, drop an assertion, add a stub, or change the setup until
  the test passes.
- A changed expectation means the behaviour changed on purpose. Say what now
  behaves differently and why, in the reply and not only in the diff.

### What to test, and how

- Every new model, controller, job, service and component needs a test.
- Do not test a helper that only renders markup from the data that it gets.
  Test a helper that has logic: a calculation, a branch, an ordering rule.
- Do not write a test that only checks that a factory is valid.
- Never stub what the test can use for real. Stub only external services:
  HTTP, third-party APIs, clocks that you cannot control. Assert on effects
  that you can see: database rows, enqueued jobs, the response.
- Controller and integration tests check the status, the redirect, the flash
  and the side effects. They never check the rendered markup. Test markup in a
  component test.
- System tests check what a user can do: click, navigate, submit, open a menu.
  A test that only checks that content is on the page belongs in a component
  test.
- In a system test, never select by a Tailwind class, and never assert styling.
  Select by a role, a label, the text, or an `id` or `data-*` attribute that
  the page already needs for its own work, such as an `aria-describedby`
  target or a Stimulus target.
- Never add an attribute, an `id` or a class to the page only for a test. If a
  test cannot find an element, select it by its role, its label or its text.

### System tests that do not flake

A flaky system test is a race between the test and the page. Find the race and
remove it. Never add `sleep`, a longer wait, or a retry.

- After a click, a submit or a `visit` that loads a new page or a Turbo
  response, wait for an element that exists only on the new page:
  `assert_selector "#user_name_error", text: "Enter a value."`. Turbo replaces
  the `body`, and a check that holds a node of the old page fails. The
  selector must match nothing on the old page: `h1` matches on both pages.
- Never put `assert_text` or `assert_no_text` right after such an action. They
  read the whole `body`, which can be the old one. Scope the text to an
  element with `assert_selector ..., text:`.
- `assert_current_path` alone does not prove the new page rendered. Turbo can
  change the URL before it replaces the `body`.
- Use only the matchers that wait: `assert_selector`, `assert_no_selector`,
  `assert_current_path`, `assert_field`, `assert_link`, `assert_button`. Never
  assert on a value that you read once, such as `find(...).text`,
  `all(...).count` or `page.has_text?`.
- To check that something went away, use `assert_no_selector` on that element.
- Run each new or changed system test three times before you commit it.

## Conventions

- Use the generators from Rails or an installed gem to make files. Write a file
  by hand only when no generator covers it. `bin/rails generate --help` lists
  them.
- RESTful controllers with the standard actions. Keep controllers thin. Put
  logic in models and services.
- ActiveRecord scopes over Ruby enumerables. Raw SQL only when necessary: say
  why, and guard against SQL injection.
- Prevent N+1 queries with `includes`. Add indexes for frequent queries.
- Order by `id`, not `created_at`.
- Use `:unprocessable_content` (422), not `:unprocessable_entity`.
- UI: build every view with `whose-components` components wherever one exists:
  buttons, forms and fields, cards, alerts, layout. Check Lookbook
  (`/lookbook`) for the existing pattern. Never hand-write Tailwind markup for
  something that `whose-components` already has. If a view needs a component
  that does not exist, use the closest one and report the gap.
- A new layout for a page never removes a function. Every link, action and
  filter on the old page must exist on the new page.
- After a UI change, click through it in a browser (Playwright) before you say
  that the task is done.

## Views

- HAML.
- Do not assign local variables at the top of a partial. Move derived values to
  a helper, a presenter or the caller.
- Never call `render` from a helper to choose a partial. Branch in the view.
- A component's own text goes in a sidecar locale file next to the component,
  for example `app/components/nav_component/nav_component.en.yml`. Read it with
  `t(".key")`. Text that is not part of a component goes in `config/locales/`.

## Ruby style

- Every Ruby file starts with `# frozen_string_literal: true`.
- Implicit hash values: `foo:`, not `foo: foo`, in hash literals and in keyword
  arguments. Fix the old form on each line that you touch.
- `filter_map` over `select` and then `map`.
- Ruby 3.4 `it`: `{ it["key"] }`, not `{ |e| e["key"] }`.

## Comments

Write no comments. That is the default, and it is almost always correct.

The test for an exception: name the wrong conclusion that the next reader makes
without the comment. If you cannot name one, write no comment.

Two kinds of comment pass the test:

- A business rule that the code cannot state.
- Behaviour that surprises: a hidden constraint, a bug workaround, a reason for
  performance or correctness that is not clear. Give a concrete example with
  real values.

Do not write a comment that:

- Says what the code does.
- Explains the change that you just made. That belongs in the commit.
- Confirms that the code works as written ("as expected", "intentional").
- Describes the task, the ticket or the pull request.
- Explains a standard Rails, Minitest or factory_bot idiom.
- Explains a Tailwind class, a layout or a style choice.

## Writing

Use the `asd-ste100` skill (in `.claude/skills/asd-ste100/`) before you write
or edit English text. Write the text that the skill returns.

- **Strict mode** for text that a machine or a user reads and acts on: error
  and flash messages, exception messages, validation messages, tool and job
  descriptions.
- **STE-flavored mode** for the rest: replies, commit messages, pull request
  titles and bodies, review comments, code comments and docs.

Replies:

- Give the result in the first sentence. Then bullets, at most four.
- Under 120 words. Code blocks do not count.
- Short, complete sentences. Ordinary words. No filler and no hedging.
- Cite `file:line` for each claim about code. Check before you assert.
- Report only a problem that you can show is reachable, and name the path.
- One topic for each reply. One recommendation, not a menu. Label each
  alternative "Option A", "Option B".
